---
// /lifecycle — the chain of custody, long-form.
//
// The longest route on the site, chosen deliberately: this page's job is not
// to convert quickly but to be the thing a sceptical reader finishes and
// believes. It is written for anyone, so it names who touches the record at
// each station and what happens there, and leaves routes and commands to the
// docs site.
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import stations from "../data/stations.json";

const title = "Lifecycle: one passport, eight stations | Odal Node";
const description =
  "One record, eight stations: declare, link, check, sign, resolve, disclose, hand over, end. Who touches the passport at each one, and what happens there.";

// Stations six to eight are the ones that only matter after the sale.
const afterSale = stations.stations.filter((s) => s.n >= 6);
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <Section eyebrow="Lifecycle" title="A passport is not a document. It is a record with a life." wide>
    <p>
      Most descriptions of a product passport stop at publication: import,
      check, sign, publish. That is the first half. A product is then repaired,
      resold, remanufactured and eventually recycled, and its record has to
      survive all of it while answering different people differently.
    </p>
    <p>
      Eight stations. Each names who touches the record there, and what
      happens to it.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section eyebrow="The spine" title="One record, eight stations." tone="muted" wide>
    <div class="not-prose space-y-5">
      {stations.stations.map((s) => (
        <article id={s.key} class={`card p-6 ${s.n >= 6 ? "card-emphasis" : ""}`}>
          <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-4 gap-y-1">
            <span class="font-mono text-sm text-neutral-400">
              {String(s.n).padStart(2, "0")}
            </span>
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-neutral-900">{s.title}</h3>
            <span class="text-sm text-neutral-500">{s.who}</span>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-3 text-base font-medium text-neutral-900">{s.lead}</p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-neutral-700">{s.body}</p>
          {s.link && (
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm">
              <a href={s.link} class="link-arrow text-primary-700">
                See it for yourself
              </a>
            </p>
          )}
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section eyebrow="After the sale" title="Publishing is only the first half." wide>
    <p>
      Import, check, sign, publish, resolve: that is a passport generator, and
      it is useful. It is also where the harder half begins.
    </p>
    <p>
      Stations {afterSale.map((s) => s.n).join(", ")} are what keep a record
      intact through years of repairs and changes of owner, rather than
      describing a product only on the day it was made:
    </p>
    <ul>
      {afterSale.map((s) => (
        <li>
          <strong>{s.title}:</strong> {s.lead}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p>
      They are why this page is long. A capability that only matters years
      after purchase cannot be shown in a screenshot, so the next best thing is
      to describe it precisely.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section eyebrow="Status" title="What is built, stated plainly." tone="tinted">
    <p>
      All eight stations exist in the software today. <strong>Nobody is running
      it in production:</strong> no customers, no pilots, no live deployments.
      The battery passport obligation begins on 18 February 2027, and we would
      rather be correct when it arrives than early and wrong.
    </p>
    <p>
      The core library is{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/odal-node/dpp-core">open source</a> under
      Apache-2.0, and anyone can read, audit or reuse it, including a
      competitor.
    </p>
  </Section>
</Base>
